# 4. RESTORE THE COMPLETE ALARM SYSTEM

The previous alarm functionality disappeared in the latest implementation.

This is a regression and must be fixed.

Restore the Alarm Manager as part of the existing Clock widget.

Do not create a second redundant Clock widget unless the existing architecture explicitly requires a separate Alarm widget.

## A. Main Clock View

Display:

- Current time.
- Date.
- Day of week.
- Optional location.
- Subtle alarm indicator when enabled alarms exist.

The alarm indicator must:
- Remain visible without hover.
- Show that alarms are configured.
- Optionally indicate the number of active alarms.
- Open the Alarm Manager when clicked.

## B. Manual Clock Navigation

Support two manually controlled views:

1. Main Clock.
2. Alarm Manager.

Use:
- Previous and next buttons.
- Horizontal swipe where supported.

There must be NO automatic slideshow.

The currently selected view must remain active until manually changed.

Remove any unwanted hover-triggered navigation controls.

## C. Alarm Creation

Users must be able to create multiple alarms.

Provide a proper time picker with:
- Hour selection.
- Minute selection.
- Clear current selection.
- Confirm action.
- Cancel action.

Do not use a plain text field as the primary time selector.

## D. Alarm Settings

Each alarm must independently support:

- Alarm label.
- Time.
- One-time or repeating schedule.
- Individual weekday selection.
- Every-day repeat.
- Specific date for one-time alarms.
- Enable/disable.
- Individual ringtone.
- Ringtone preview.
- Volume.
- Individual snooze duration.

Available snooze presets:

- 5 minutes.
- 10 minutes.
- 15 minutes.
- 20 minutes.
- 30 minutes.
- Custom duration.

## E. Multiple Alarms

Allow unlimited practical user-created alarms.

Provide:
- Add.
- Edit.
- Delete.
- Enable.
- Disable.

If multiple alarms become due simultaneously, do not discard or overwrite any of them.

Handle them through a controlled notification and playback queue.

## F. Ringtone

Allow selection from:
- Available system sounds.
- User-selected local audio files.

Provide:
- Preview.
- Stop preview.
- Volume.
- Per-alarm ringtone selection.

Do not bundle large sound collections.

Do not download sounds automatically.

## G. Alarm Scheduler

The Rust backend must be responsible for reliable alarm scheduling.

Use the existing shared backend.

Do not create a new daemon for alarms.

The scheduler must:
- Persist alarms.
- Calculate the next alarm.
- Sleep until relevant events.
- Recalculate after changes.
- Handle timezone and clock changes.
- Recheck after system suspend/resume.
- Avoid excessive polling.

Alarms must work when the Clock widget is not currently visible, provided the existing background service is running and the user session is active.

Do not claim alarms can ring while the computer is completely powered off.

## H. Regression Prevention

Restoring alarms must not break the existing Clock appearance.

Keep the main Clock view and Alarm Manager connected through a stable, manually controlled navigation system.

