# 8. PHOTO FRAME — ACTUAL CROP AND FIXED IMAGE POSITIONING

The previous Photo Frame implementation is missing the crop/fix image functionality.

This feature must now be implemented fully.

## A. Display Modes

Support:

1. Single Photo.
2. Slideshow.

Single Photo:
- Display one selected image.
- No automatic image rotation.
- Allow manual image selection.

Slideshow:
- Rotate through selected images.
- Enable/disable slideshow.
- Configure image interval.

## B. Interval

Presets:
- 5 seconds.
- 10 seconds.
- 15 seconds.
- 30 seconds.
- 60 seconds.
- Custom.

Persist the interval.

Do not run a slideshow timer while the mode is disabled.

## C. Interactive Crop Editor

Implement a real crop editor.

The editor must provide:

- Actual live image preview.
- Image dragging.
- Horizontal positioning.
- Vertical positioning.
- Zoom.
- Reset.
- Confirm.
- Cancel.

The preview must use the actual widget aspect ratio.

When the widget displays an image in Cover mode, the crop editor must accurately show which area will remain visible.

Users must be able to select the exact part of the image they want to display.

## D. Fixed Crop Configuration

Save crop configuration individually for each image.

Store:
- Image path or stable identifier.
- Scale.
- Horizontal position.
- Vertical position.
- Fit mode.
- Widget aspect ratio where necessary.

Do not destructively alter the original image file.

Changing widget size should not silently discard the user's crop configuration.

Provide a reset option.

## E. Preview Accuracy

The preview must match the final desktop widget as closely as possible.

Do not create a fake crop preview that merely displays the complete image.

Do not omit the actual drag and positioning interaction.

## F. Supported Formats

Verify real support for:
- SVG.
- JPG.
- JPEG.
- PNG.
- WebP.

Retain the existing custom background functionality.

Use lazy loading and image caching to maintain performance.

