# 9. MUSIC PLAYER — FINISH THE ACTUAL FUNCTIONALITY

The current Music Player widget exists visually, but the source configuration functionality is incomplete.

It is not acceptable for v3.0.1.

The user must be able to select where music comes from.

Implement two complete source modes.

## A. Source Selection Interface

Add a clearly accessible configuration section called:

Music Source

Provide two choices:

1. Local Music.
2. Online Stream.

These choices must actually work.

## B. Local Music Mode

Users must be able to:

1. Open a native folder picker.
2. Select a local music directory.
3. Scan supported audio files.
4. Display discovered tracks.
5. Select a track.
6. Start playback.
7. Pause.
8. Resume.
9. Stop.
10. Navigate next/previous.
11. Seek within a track.
12. Adjust volume.

Display:
- Track title.
- Artist.
- Album.
- Duration.
- Album artwork where available.

The user must be able to change the selected music folder.

Provide a manual rescan action.

Avoid repeatedly rescanning the folder.

Cache metadata where appropriate.

Do not scan the entire home directory automatically.

## C. Online Stream Mode

Create a real URL input.

Users must be able to:

1. Enter a direct playable audio or stream URL.
2. Validate the URL.
3. Connect to the stream.
4. Start playback.
5. Pause if supported.
6. Stop.
7. Save favorite URLs.
8. Select saved URLs.
9. Remove saved URLs.

Support direct audio files and compatible internet radio streams where the chosen playback backend permits.

IMPORTANT:

A normal website URL is not automatically a direct audio URL.

Do not claim that YouTube, Spotify, or other protected streaming page URLs are playable unless an explicitly supported integration exists.

Do not bypass DRM or access controls.

Do not scrape protected streaming services.

Provide clear error feedback when a URL is unsupported.

## D. Audio Playback Must Be Real

Do not implement fake play buttons.

Do not simulate moving progress bars without real playback.

Use an actual compatible playback backend.

Before adding dependencies, inspect the existing environment.

Prefer reusing existing Linux multimedia infrastructure.

Evaluate:
- Existing project audio library.
- GStreamer availability.
- Qt Multimedia availability.
- Existing MPRIS capabilities.

Choose one suitable solution rather than several overlapping engines.

## E. Playback State

The widget must receive actual playback state updates.

Display:
- Playing.
- Paused.
- Stopped.
- Buffering.
- Error.

Update track metadata immediately after track changes.

Do not require widget resize or restart.

## F. Playback Queue

Provide:
- Add track to queue.
- Remove track.
- Clear queue.
- Select track.
- Next.
- Previous.

Keep queue functionality simple and reliable.

## G. Music Player Appearance

Use the same URWidgets visual design system.

Suggested layout:

- Artwork on the left.
- Title and artist on the right.
- Previous, play/pause, next controls.
- Progress bar.
- Current time and duration.

Apply the real shared shadow effect.

Respect the Music Player's independent Appearance preferences.

